# Python (Twitch → Telegram bot)

Source: adapted from [awesome-cursorrules](https://github.com/PatrickJS/awesome-cursorrules) Python best practices / projects guide — scoped to this repo.

## Stack

- Python 3.13, `python-telegram-bot` (async), `requests`, SQLite locally / PostgreSQL via `DATABASE_URL` + `psycopg`
- Flat modules at repo root (`bot.py`, `db.py`, `config.py`, …) — do not invent a `src/` layout
- Config only from env (`.env` / `.env.example`); never commit secrets
- Checks: prefer existing `self_check.py` over adding a full pytest tree unless asked

## Style

- Match surrounding file: snake_case, early returns, async handlers as `async def`
- Type hints where the file already uses them; do not mass-annotate untouched code
- No new dependencies without explicit approval
- Keep handlers small; reuse helpers already in `bot.py` / `db.py` / `i18n.py`

## Safety

- Parameterized SQL only (`db.py` patterns); no string-built queries
- Do not log tokens, OAuth codes, or raw `.env` values
- Validate Telegram/Twitch user input at trust boundaries
